`,s.jsx(F,{items:[{q:"Can you overfit a random forest by adding trees, and can you overfit boosting that way?",a:"Not the forest: extra trees only refine an average over independent fits, so the score plateaus rather than degrading. Boosting can, because each round fits what is left over, and once the signal is exhausted the remaining residual is noise. That is why boosting needs early stopping on a validation set and a forest does not."}]}),`
`,s.jsx(e.div,{className:"lesson-visual",role:"region","aria-label":"Hyperparameter interactive example; scroll horizontally if needed",tabIndex:"0",children:s.jsx(as,{})}),`
`,s.jsx(F,{items:[{q:"Why leave max_depth=None for a random forest but never for a lone tree?",a:"A single deep tree's variance is the model's variance. In a forest, averaging across decorrelated trees removes most of that variance, so depth buys you low bias almost for free — capping it would just add bias with no compensating benefit. The ensemble, not the individual tree, is the regularised object."},{q:"Why is max_features the highest-leverage RF knob?",a:"Because variance is rho*sigma^2 + (1-rho)*sigma^2/B, and only max_features moves rho. Everything else — more trees, bigger leaves — attacks terms that either vanish quickly or trade against bias. Reducing m decorrelates the trees, lowering the irreducible floor, at the cost of making each individual tree slightly worse."},{q:"You halve learning_rate from 0.1 to 0.05 and keep n_estimators fixed. Predict the result.",a:"Underfitting. Effective capacity is roughly eta times M, so halving eta halves how far the ensemble travels from its starting point in the same number of steps — it stops before reaching the fit it previously achieved. To keep capacity constant you must roughly double M."},{q:"Why does gradient boosting need early stopping while random forests essentially don't?",a:"RF trees are independent, so adding more only averages away noise — test error flattens rather than rising. GB trees are conditioned on their predecessors, so each iteration adds capacity aimed at the current residuals, which eventually means fitting noise. Test error therefore has a minimum and rises after it, and early stopping on a validation set is what finds that point."},{q:"Train error low, test error high, on a gradient boosting model. Name three knobs, in order.",a:"First lower n_estimators via early stopping — the most direct capacity reduction. Then reduce max_depth, since shallow trees fit interactions less aggressively. Then add subsampling of rows or columns, or increase the L2 regularisation term, to inject randomness and shrink leaf weights. Raising the learning rate would be counterproductive."}]}),`
